  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nEarly morning surface observations across eastern and north central \nKansas reflect a departing surface ridge axis to the east and gradual\nlee troughing developing across the High Plains. \n\nThrough Thursday, the large-scale pattern remains amplified with \nmean upper-level troughing anchored over the western CONUS and \ndownstream ridging building across the Plains and eastern U.S. \nPersistent surface pressure falls in the lee of the Rockies will \ntighten the pressure gradient across the area. Southerly winds will \nincrease to 10 to 20 mph today with gusts of 25 to 40 mph, \nparticularly across north central Kansas (Concordia, Belleville, \nAbilene) and the Flint Hills. Warm air advection and deeper boundary-\nlayer mixing will lift highs into the upper 50s to low 60s, warming \nfurther into the low to mid 60s Wednesday and mid 60s Thursday. \nPassing mid and high-level cloud cover from western shortwave energy \nwill yield partly to mostly cloudy periods tonight into Wednesday \nmorning before clearing, keeping overnight lows elevated in the upper\n30s to mid 40s.\n\nBy Friday, the western upper trough and associated Pacific energy \neject eastward into the Rockies and Plains. Ahead of an approaching \nsurface cold front, a strengthening low-level mass response will \ndrive the windiest conditions of the period on Friday afternoon, with\nsustained southerly winds of 20 to 25 mph and gusts up to 45 mph \nacross north-central Kansas. Highs will peak in the mid to upper 60s\nahead of the boundary. Despite steady southerly moisture return, \ncured dormant fuels and gusty winds warrant continued monitoring of \nafternoon fire weather conditions through Friday, though rising dew \npoints should temper critical relative humidity overlap.\n\nThe cold front sweeps across northeast and east-central Kansas \nFriday night into Saturday morning. Deeper moisture return remains \nmodest and primary synoptic-scale ascent lifts largely north and west\nof the area, limiting precipitation potential to a 25 percent chance\nof light showers after midnight Friday night through Saturday \nmorning. Medium to long range guidance indicates another progressive\ntrough emerging from the western U.S. by Monday.",
